of their aunties to a nearby park. By the time evening fell, the two of them had decided to go into town. Burke was a small town with a population of just 3,400 people. It was originally home to the Namba people, but when white settlers arrived in the area during the mid-1800s, these traditional landholders were dispossessed. Like all indigenous people across Australia, the Namba people battled a loss of land and culture, were hit hard by European disease, and experienced conflict with colonists. By the late 19th century, their population had dwindled.
